The longest-standing incorrect proof of the Four Color Theorem in graph theory is attributed to Alfred Kempe, a British barrister who published his "proof" in 1879 in the Philosophical Magazine. Initially, his proof was hailed as a solution to the problem, and it was even mentioned in an 1880 speech by mathematician William Kingdon Clifford in which he claimed that Kempe's paper marked the "end of an era" in mathematics.

However, a problem with Kempe's proof was identified by Percy John Heawood in 1890. Heawood showed that Kempe's argument failed in a specific class of configurations called "diagrams," and he was able to construct a counterexample in which five colors were needed instead of four. This line of reasoning eventually led to the proof of the Four Color Theorem in its modern form, presented independently by Wolfgang Haken and Kenneth Appel in 1976 and by Kenneth Appel and Wolfgang Haken in 1977.

After Heawood's discovery, Kempe revised his proof twice, in 1880 and 1883, but neither revision was successful in correcting the fundamental flaw in his original proof. Heawood's counterexample highlights a situation where it's not possible to correctly color a particular configuration of regions using only four colors, demonstrating the complexity of the Four Color Theorem. From then on, the proof of the Four Color Theorem was pursued by researchers like Kempe, Heawood, Haken, Appel, as well as others, and the final correct proof was achieved through the use of advanced computer algorithms.<|end_of_text|>

